  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nCurrent early morning polar satellite generated ALPW layer imagery \nshows a few embedded waves traveling northward with-in plume of \ndeeper moisture streaming northward from Mexico into New Mexico/west \nTexas/western Kansas. Periods of showers and occasional embedded \nstorms will continue to stream northward across western Kansas and \nlocations generally west of I-135 in central Kansas today with-in \nthe rich precipitable water axis. Strong or severe thunderstorm \npotential will remain low given limited instability and poor mid-\nlevel lapse rates. The richer precipitable water axis will gradually \nshift eastward for tonight into Friday with ON/OFF periods of \nshowers/embedded storms persisting over central/south central \nKansas. Locally heavy rainfall is possible given rich atmospheric \nmoisture with some brief periods of training showers/storms \ncontaining higher rainfall rates for tonight into Friday. HREF shows \nan uptick of surface based instability occurring for locations west \nof a Anthony to Kingman, Great Bend line where a few strong storms \ncould try and materialize Friday afternoon. Loitering \nclouds/precipitation for locations east of that line will likely \nkeep the low levels stabilized and potential for severe weather low. \nThis could also result in cooler than current forecast daytime highs \nfor Friday. The last remaining upper level wave looks eject from the \nRockies into Kansas for late Friday night into Saturday resulting in \na continuation of showers/storms over the region. Models are showing \nbetter potential for some clearing skies and modest surface \nheating/increased instability levels occurring Saturday afternoon \nover western/central Kansas. Depending on timing of upper level wave \nthis could become an area for surface based storms to initiate with \na few strong storms possibly affecting our CWA.\n\nA lull in the activity is expected for Sunday-Monday as upper level \nridging briefly amplifies over the region. Meanwhile heading into \nthe Tuesday-Wednesday period models continue to show a more \npronounced upper level wave ejecting from the Rockies into the \ncentral plains. GEFS model ensembles are highlighting a healthy \ndeepening/slowing down trend of this upper level wave. The \ningredients coming together predicted by the latest model \nguidance shows signals for possible severe storms given \ncombination of increasing wind shear/instability. However, if \nthe model trends continue to deepen and slow the speed of this \nupper level wave down the scenario could transition to more of a\nheavy rainfall/flooding threat for the region.",
